Device controller adapter construction
======================================

The legacy device adapter helper now uses build_adapter_for_controller, the
same builder used elsewhere for stored controller authentication. Proxmox token
ID/secret and custom password realm reach the adapter; tokens retain precedence
over ticket/password login. Omada cloud fields, local credentials, ports and
explicit TLS verification settings are preserved. The old helper dropped the
Proxmox settings even though other paths supported them.

Invalid stored secrets and unexpected constructor failures return a fixed 502
Controller adapter unavailable, without raw error text in that response or the
helper's warning. Construction stops before using a corrupt credential. Existing
AdapterError policy handling and cancellation propagate unchanged; a missing
controller remains 400. Two catch-and-pass decryption fallbacks are removed.
Real Controller password/client-secret properties already raised on corrupted
ciphertext before the old local try block: no plaintext/ciphertext exfiltration
through the real ORM model was demonstrated by this qualification.

Tests use actual PostgreSQL Controller/Device rows, encrypted and legacy secret
forms, token-versus-password precedence, default/custom realms, TLS settings,
cloud fields, wrong-key/malformed ciphertext, constructor failures, adapter
policy errors and cancellation. Focused client tests use MockTransport. The
independent probe uses actual Proxmox adapter/client code against an isolated
TLS socket server, a fixture CA and an explicit loopback URL override. It checks
both the shared factory control and the corrected device path, plus failed token
authentication and HTTP client cleanup. No hardware, caller login, actual device
command or concurrent authority revocation is exercised.

This construction correction does not implement generic Proxmox device reboot,
firmware or other methods that the adapter does not support. Those retain their
existing unsupported behavior; hypervisor operation routes are separate. It also
does not qualify referenced-controller lifecycle/tenant consistency or all batch
error diagnostics. Full shipping/runtime acceptance is separately recorded.
